==English==

===Etymology===
Compare Irish and Gaelic {{m|ga|gog}}, a nod, a slight motion.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈɡɒ.ɡəl/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɒɡəl}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|goggl}}

# To [[stare]] (at something) with [[wide]] [[eye]]s.
#* {{RQ:Wodehouse Offing|chapter=IV, XII, and XV|passage=[...] she frowned a displeased frown and told me for heaven's sake to stop '''goggling''' like a dead halibut. [...]<!--ch. XII--> She left me fogged and groping for the inner meaning, and I could see from Aunt Dahlia's '''goggling''' eyes that the basic idea hadn't got across with her either. <!--ch. XV-->[...] I didn't want to be hampered by an audience. When you're pushing someone into a lake, nothing embarrasses you more than having the front seats filled up with '''goggling''' spectators.}}
# To [[roll]] the eyes.
#* ''Hudibras''
#*: And wink and '''goggle''' like an owl.

